    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Catherine presents with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing loss. Rinne test is negative (BC > AC) bilaterally. Weber test does not lateralize. Audiogram shows a bilateral conductive hearing loss, with a specific drop in bone conduction thresholds at 2000 Hz (**Carhart''s notch**). Otoscopy is completely normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Presbycusis; age-related sensorineural degeneration of the auditory nerve and sensory cochlear structures", "Cholesteatoma; a destructive, keratinizing epithelial lesion of the middle ear or mastoid cavity", "Eustachian tube dysfunction; chronic negative middle ear pressure with retraction pocket and fluid accumulation", "Otosclerosis; a metabolic bone disease characterized by abnormal bone remodeling of the otic capsule, leading to fixation of the stapes footplate", "Temporal arteritis; autoimmune vasculitis causing ischemia of the cochlea and stria vascularis"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Otosclerosis** is a metabolic bone disease of the otic capsule characterized by abnormal bone resorption and deposition. It classically leads to **fixation of the stapes footplate** in the oval window, causing progressive conductive hearing loss. Key diagnostic features: (1) Bilateral conductive hearing loss (Rinne negative BC > AC), (2) Normal tympanic membrane (ruling out otitis media or cholesteatoma), and (3) **Carhart''s notch** (a characteristic artificial dip in bone conduction thresholds at 2000 Hz due to loss of the normal stapes resonance). While typically presenting in young adulthood, otosclerosis can progress or present in older adults. Treatment includes hearing aids or stapedectomy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 87-year-old female named Florence presents with slowly progressive, bilateral hearing loss. Rinne test is negative (BC > AC) bilaterally. Weber test does not lateralize. Audiogram shows a bilateral conductive hearing loss, with a specific drop in bone conduction thresholds at 2000 Hz (**Carhart''s notch**). Otoscopy is completely normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Temporal arteritis; autoimmune vasculitis causing ischemia of the cochlea and stria vascularis", "Eustachian tube dysfunction; chronic negative middle ear pressure with retraction pocket and fluid accumulation", "Cholesteatoma; a destructive, keratinizing epithelial lesion of the middle ear or mastoid cavity", "Presbycusis; age-related sensorineural degeneration of the auditory nerve and sensory cochlear structures", "Otosclerosis; a metabolic bone disease characterized by abnormal bone remodeling of the otic capsule, leading to fixation of the stapes footplate"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Otosclerosis** is a metabolic bone disease of the otic capsule characterized by abnormal bone resorption and deposition. It classically leads to **fixation of the stapes footplate** in the oval window, causing progressive conductive hearing loss. Key diagnostic features: (1) Bilateral conductive hearing loss (Rinne negative BC > AC), (2) Normal tympanic membrane (ruling out otitis media or cholesteatoma), and (3) **Carhart''s notch** (a characteristic artificial dip in bone conduction thresholds at 2000 Hz due to loss of the normal stapes resonance). While typically presenting in young adulthood, otosclerosis can progress or present in older adults. Treatment includes hearing aids or stapedectomy. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old male named Donald presents with a sudden loss of hearing in the right ear that occurred over 2 hours yesterday morning. He reports associated high-pitched tinnitus and mild vertigo. There is no history of trauma or ototoxic exposures. Otoscopy is normal. Weber test lateralizes to the **opposite** ear. Rinne test is positive (AC > BC) bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Start empiric oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for acute otitis media and advise hydrogen peroxide ear drops to clear cerumen", "Initiate high-dose oral glucocorticoids (e.g., prednisone 60 mg daily for 10-14 days) immediately, and refer to an otolaryngologist for urgent evaluation; this is a medical emergency", "Order an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the temporal bone and defer all medications until the imaging is reviewed", "Prescribe a 5-day course of oral acyclovir, as the condition is universally caused by herpes simplex virus reactivation and steroids are contraindicated", "Reassure the patient that this is a normal age-related hearing change (presbycusis) and schedule a routine audiology referral in 6 months"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)** is defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss of >= 30 decibels affecting >= 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours. It is a **medical emergency**. The Weber test lateralizing to the *opposite (normal) ear* and Rinne test *positive (AC > BC)* confirms a sensorineural deficit on the affected side. The etiology is idiopathic in most cases (post-viral or microvascular). **Immediate initiation of high-dose oral corticosteroids** (prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, or intratympanic steroid injections) within 2 weeks of onset significantly improves the rate of hearing recovery. Deferring treatment leads to permanent hearing loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old male named Lawrence presents with a sudden loss of hearing in the left ear that occurred over 3 hours yesterday morning. He reports associated high-pitched tinnitus and mild vertigo. There is no history of trauma or ototoxic exposures. Otoscopy is normal. Weber test lateralizes to the **opposite** ear. Rinne test is positive (AC > BC) bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Reassure the patient that this is a normal age-related hearing change (presbycusis) and schedule a routine audiology referral in 6 months", "Order an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the temporal bone and defer all medications until the imaging is reviewed", "Prescribe a 5-day course of oral acyclovir, as the condition is universally caused by herpes simplex virus reactivation and steroids are contraindicated", "Initiate high-dose oral glucocorticoids (e.g., prednisone 60 mg daily for 10-14 days) immediately, and refer to an otolaryngologist for urgent evaluation; this is a medical emergency", "Start empiric oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for acute otitis media and advise hydrogen peroxide ear drops to clear cerumen"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)** is defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss of >= 30 decibels affecting >= 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours. It is a **medical emergency**. The Weber test lateralizing to the *opposite (normal) ear* and Rinne test *positive (AC > BC)* confirms a sensorineural deficit on the affected side. The etiology is idiopathic in most cases (post-viral or microvascular). **Immediate initiation of high-dose oral corticosteroids** (prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, or intratympanic steroid injections) within 2 weeks of onset significantly improves the rate of hearing recovery. Deferring treatment leads to permanent hearing loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
