    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old female named Jean presents with a sudden loss of hearing in the left ear that occurred over 4 hours yesterday morning. She reports associated high-pitched tinnitus and mild vertigo. There is no history of trauma or ototoxic exposures. Otoscopy is normal. Weber test lateralizes to the **opposite** ear. Rinne test is positive (AC > BC) bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Initiate high-dose oral glucocorticoids (e.g., prednisone 60 mg daily for 10-14 days) immediately, and refer to an otolaryngologist for urgent evaluation; this is a medical emergency", "Order an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the temporal bone and defer all medications until the imaging is reviewed", "Prescribe a 5-day course of oral acyclovir, as the condition is universally caused by herpes simplex virus reactivation and steroids are contraindicated", "Reassure the patient that this is a normal age-related hearing change (presbycusis) and schedule a routine audiology referral in 6 months", "Start empiric oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for acute otitis media and advise hydrogen peroxide ear drops to clear cerumen"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)** is defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss of >= 30 decibels affecting >= 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours. It is a **medical emergency**. The Weber test lateralizing to the *opposite (normal) ear* and Rinne test *positive (AC > BC)* confirms a sensorineural deficit on the affected side. The etiology is idiopathic in most cases (post-viral or microvascular). **Immediate initiation of high-dose oral corticosteroids** (prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, or intratympanic steroid injections) within 2 weeks of onset significantly improves the rate of hearing recovery. Deferring treatment leads to permanent hearing loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old female named Clara presents with a sudden loss of hearing in the right ear that occurred over 2 hours yesterday morning. She reports associated high-pitched tinnitus and mild vertigo. There is no history of trauma or ototoxic exposures. Otoscopy is normal. Weber test lateralizes to the **opposite** ear. Rinne test is positive (AC > BC) bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Order an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the temporal bone and defer all medications until the imaging is reviewed", "Reassure the patient that this is a normal age-related hearing change (presbycusis) and schedule a routine audiology referral in 6 months", "Initiate high-dose oral glucocorticoids (e.g., prednisone 60 mg daily for 10-14 days) immediately, and refer to an otolaryngologist for urgent evaluation; this is a medical emergency", "Prescribe a 5-day course of oral acyclovir, as the condition is universally caused by herpes simplex virus reactivation and steroids are contraindicated", "Start empiric oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for acute otitis media and advise hydrogen peroxide ear drops to clear cerumen"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)** is defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss of >= 30 decibels affecting >= 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours. It is a **medical emergency**. The Weber test lateralizing to the *opposite (normal) ear* and Rinne test *positive (AC > BC)* confirms a sensorineural deficit on the affected side. The etiology is idiopathic in most cases (post-viral or microvascular). **Immediate initiation of high-dose oral corticosteroids** (prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, or intratympanic steroid injections) within 2 weeks of onset significantly improves the rate of hearing recovery. Deferring treatment leads to permanent hearing loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old male named John presents with a sudden loss of hearing in the right ear that occurred over 4 hours yesterday morning. He reports associated high-pitched tinnitus and mild vertigo. There is no history of trauma or ototoxic exposures. Otoscopy is normal. Weber test lateralizes to the **opposite** ear. Rinne test is positive (AC > BC) bilaterally. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Reassure the patient that this is a normal age-related hearing change (presbycusis) and schedule a routine audiology referral in 6 months", "Prescribe a 5-day course of oral acyclovir, as the condition is universally caused by herpes simplex virus reactivation and steroids are contraindicated", "Start empiric oral amoxicillin-clavulanate for acute otitis media and advise hydrogen peroxide ear drops to clear cerumen", "Order an urgent non-contrast CT scan of the temporal bone and defer all medications until the imaging is reviewed", "Initiate high-dose oral glucocorticoids (e.g., prednisone 60 mg daily for 10-14 days) immediately, and refer to an otolaryngologist for urgent evaluation; this is a medical emergency"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Sudden sensorineural hearing loss (SSNHL)** is defined as a rapid-onset sensorineural hearing loss of >= 30 decibels affecting >= 3 contiguous frequencies within 72 hours. It is a **medical emergency**. The Weber test lateralizing to the *opposite (normal) ear* and Rinne test *positive (AC > BC)* confirms a sensorineural deficit on the affected side. The etiology is idiopathic in most cases (post-viral or microvascular). **Immediate initiation of high-dose oral corticosteroids** (prednisone 1 mg/kg/day, up to 60 mg, or intratympanic steroid injections) within 2 weeks of onset significantly improves the rate of hearing recovery. Deferring treatment leads to permanent hearing loss. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 81-year-old female named Dorothy with severe presbycusis presents with progressive gait instability and recurrent falls, especially in low-light environments. Neurological examination reveals normal strength, reflexes, and proprioception. However, the patient has a wide-based gait and sways significantly during the Romberg test **only** when eyes are closed. What is the most likely pathophysiological link between her hearing loss and her balance deficit?', '["Concurrent age-related degeneration of vestibular hair cells and otoconia in the saccule and utricle (presbyb equilibrium/presbyastasis), compounding the sensory deficit of presbycusis", "Diabetic peripheral neuropathy causing sensory ataxia due to demyelination of the dorsal columns of the spinal cord", "Excessive accumulation of endolymph within the cochlea leading to mechanical blockage of the Eustachian tube", "Hearing aids causing chronic visual vestibular mismatch by vibrating the ossicular chain and semicircular canals", "A localized cerebellar vermis infarct that selectively damages high-frequency auditory inputs and vestibulospinal reflexes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Age-related hearing loss (presbycusis) often co-occurs with age-related vestibular decline (**presbyastasis** or presbyb equilibrium) because both cochlear and vestibular structures reside in the inner ear and share common arterial supply (internal auditory artery) and pathways. Pathophysiology includes: (1) Degeneration of hair cells in the semicircular canals, saccule, and utricle, (2) Loss of vestibular nerve fibers, and (3) Reduction in otoconia. This vestibular deficit impairs the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR) and vestibulospinal reflex, causing gait instability. In low light, the loss of visual compensation worsens the instability, leading to falls. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Current Geriatrics)'),
