    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old male named George with severe presbycusis has recently moved to a long-term care facility. The nursing staff notes that the patient often withdraws from conversations, appears confused during medical reviews, and is suspected of having cognitive impairment. What is the most appropriate first-line communication strategy for the staff to implement?', '["Increase the speed of speech to keep the auditory cortex stimulated and prevent central cognitive processing delays", "Shout directly into the patient''s ear at a high volume and high pitch to overcome the high-frequency hearing loss", "Stand behind the patient while speaking to prevent the patient from relying on lip-reading, which impairs central auditory adaptation", "Communicate exclusively through written notes, as older adults with presbycusis cannot process any spoken words", "Face the patient directly at eye level, minimize background noise, speak slowly and clearly in a slightly lower pitch/tone, and use concise sentences; avoid shouting"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Proper communication techniques are essential when interacting with older adults with presbycusis: (1) **Face the patient directly** to allow visual cues (speech-reading/lip-reading) and gestures, (2) **Minimize background noise** (turn off TV, close door), (3) **Speak slowly and clearly**, (4) **Lower the pitch of your voice** (low-frequency hearing is preserved, while high-frequency hearing is lost), (5) **Do NOT shout** (shouting distorts the acoustic signal, increases the pitch of the voice, and makes speech even harder to understand). These strategies help prevent misdiagnosing hearing loss as cognitive decline or dementia. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
