    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Herbert with long-standing bilateral presbycusis presents with a 6-month history of continuous, high-pitched, bilateral ringing in his ears. The tinnitus is most bothersome at night in a quiet bedroom, causing insomnia and anxiety. There are no focal neurological deficits. What is the most appropriate initial management strategy?', '["Prescribe a daily long-acting benzodiazepine (e.g., clorazepate or flurazepam) at bedtime as first-line therapy to suppress the auditory signal", "Reassure the patient, recommend sound masking (e.g., white noise machines, fans, or hearing aids that amplify ambient noise during the day), and initiate cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) to reduce tinnitus-associated distress", "Recommend absolute silence in the bedroom and avoiding all background sounds to allow the auditory system to rest", "Start the patient on high-dose oral aminoglycosides to chemically ablate the hyperactive cochlear hair cells", "Refer for immediate intracranial angiogram to rule out a high-flow arteriovenous malformation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Tinnitus is highly prevalent in older adults with presbycusis. It represents a central auditory neuroplastic response to peripheral sensory deprivation (cochlear deafferentation). Management focuses on **habituation and distress reduction**: (1) **Reassurance** (tinnitus is rarely a sign of serious disease), (2) **Sound masking** (white noise, fans, or hearing aids, which amplify ambient noise and decrease the brain''s focus on the internal phantom sound), and (3) **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)**, which is the most evidence-based approach for tinnitus-related distress. Benzodiazepines are **Beers Criteria drugs** and should be avoided due to fall and delirium risk. Aminoglycosides are ototoxic and will worsen hearing loss and tinnitus. (Ref: AAO-HNS Clinical Practice Guideline; Hazzard''s Ch. 45; AGS Beers Criteria 2023)'),
