    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Roy presents with blurred near vision and eye strain. He reports holding reading materials at arm''s length to see clearly. What is the primary underlying pathophysiology of this universal, age-related ocular change?', '["Progressive shortening of the anteroposterior diameter of the globe, causing light to focus behind the retina", "Progressive stiffening and loss of elasticity of the lens nucleus and capsule, combined with age-related functional decline of the ciliary muscle, leading to a loss of accommodative power", "Increased resistance to aqueous humor outflow through the trabecular meshwork", "Progressive clouding and opacification of the crystalline lens matrix due to protein denaturation", "Degeneration of the photoreceptor cells within the fovea centralis of the macula lutea"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Presbyopia** is the universal age-related loss of **accommodative amplitude** (the ability to focus on near objects). The primary pathophysiology involves: (1) **Loss of elasticity** and progressive stiffening of the lens capsule and lens nucleus (lens sclerosis), making it less able to become spherical, and (2) **Functional decline of the ciliary muscle**. When ciliary muscle contracts, zonules relax, and a young elastic lens naturally rounds up to increase refractive power. An older stiffened lens cannot change shape, so light from near objects focuses *behind* the retina rather than *on* it. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old male named Paul presents with blurred near vision and eye strain. He reports holding reading materials at arm''s length to see clearly. What is the primary underlying pathophysiology of this universal, age-related ocular change?', '["Progressive stiffening and loss of elasticity of the lens nucleus and capsule, combined with age-related functional decline of the ciliary muscle, leading to a loss of accommodative power", "Progressive shortening of the anteroposterior diameter of the globe, causing light to focus behind the retina", "Degeneration of the photoreceptor cells within the fovea centralis of the macula lutea", "Progressive clouding and opacification of the crystalline lens matrix due to protein denaturation", "Increased resistance to aqueous humor outflow through the trabecular meshwork"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Presbyopia** is the universal age-related loss of **accommodative amplitude** (the ability to focus on near objects). The primary pathophysiology involves: (1) **Loss of elasticity** and progressive stiffening of the lens capsule and lens nucleus (lens sclerosis), making it less able to become spherical, and (2) **Functional decline of the ciliary muscle**. When ciliary muscle contracts, zonules relax, and a young elastic lens naturally rounds up to increase refractive power. An older stiffened lens cannot change shape, so light from near objects focuses *behind* the retina rather than *on* it. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
