    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Herbert is diagnosed with presbyopia. His distance vision is completely normal (20/20), but he requires correction for reading. Which of the following lens types is indicated to correct his near vision, and what is its optical mechanism?', '["Concave (minus) lenses; they diverge incoming light rays, shifting the focal point backward to compensate for an abnormally elongated globe", "Convex (plus) lenses; they converge incoming light rays before they enter the eye, shifting the focal point forward onto the retina to compensate for the loss of natural lens accommodation", "Photochromic lenses; they filter high-intensity ultraviolet light to prevent corneal edema and pupil constriction", "Prism lenses; they shift the visual field to correct binocular diplopia and ocular misalignment", "Cylindrical lenses; they correct focal disparities along different meridians of the cornea and anterior chamber"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Presbyopia causes the near point of accommodation to recede, meaning light from near objects focuses behind the retina. To correct near vision in a patient with normal distance vision (emmetropia), **convex (plus) lenses** are prescribed. Convex lenses are converging lenses: they bend light rays inward before they reach the cornea, increasing the refractive power of the optical system and shifting the focal point forward onto the fovea. Concave lenses are diverging lenses used to correct myopia (nearsightedness). Cylindrical lenses correct astigmatism. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
