    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Herbert with a history of hypertension and osteoarthritis is prescribed multifocal glasses to manage concurrent presbyopia and myopia. He presents to the clinic after sustaining a fall while walking down his basement stairs. He reports that he misjudged the height of the step. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical reasoning regarding the relationship between his eyewear and his fall?', '["Multifocal lenses cause chronic pupillary constriction, which impairs dark adaptation and leads to sudden autonomic syncope on stairs", "The lower segment of multifocal/bifocal lenses is designed for near vision; looking through it while walking distorts depth perception and spatial awareness of the lower visual field (e.g., stairs, curbs), increasing fall risk", "The fall was unrelated to the glasses and was entirely caused by age-related peripheral motor apraxia", "The patient should wear stronger reading glasses while walking to improve visual acuity of distant stairs and obstacles", "Multifocal glasses cause transient horizontal nystagmus by stimulating the vestibular system during vertical eye movements"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Multifocal, bifocal, and progressive lenses** are a major, often overlooked, modifiable risk factor for falls in older adults. The lower portion of these lenses is corrected for near vision (reading distance). When an older adult looks down through the bottom of the lens while walking or navigating stairs, the ground and steps appear **blurred and distorted**, which severely **impairs depth perception and spatial contrast sensitivity**. The **Visible Trial** demonstrated that providing **single-lens distance glasses for outdoor walking and stair navigation** in older adults who habitually wore multifocals reduced fall rates by 40%. (Ref: Visible Trial, BMJ 2010; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old male named Howard with a history of hypertension and osteoarthritis is prescribed progressive glasses to manage concurrent presbyopia and myopia. He presents to the clinic after sustaining a fall while walking down his basement stairs. He reports that he misjudged the height of the step. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical reasoning regarding the relationship between his eyewear and his fall?', '["Multifocal lenses cause chronic pupillary constriction, which impairs dark adaptation and leads to sudden autonomic syncope on stairs", "Multifocal glasses cause transient horizontal nystagmus by stimulating the vestibular system during vertical eye movements", "The lower segment of multifocal/bifocal lenses is designed for near vision; looking through it while walking distorts depth perception and spatial awareness of the lower visual field (e.g., stairs, curbs), increasing fall risk", "The fall was unrelated to the glasses and was entirely caused by age-related peripheral motor apraxia", "The patient should wear stronger reading glasses while walking to improve visual acuity of distant stairs and obstacles"]'::jsonb, 2, '**Multifocal, bifocal, and progressive lenses** are a major, often overlooked, modifiable risk factor for falls in older adults. The lower portion of these lenses is corrected for near vision (reading distance). When an older adult looks down through the bottom of the lens while walking or navigating stairs, the ground and steps appear **blurred and distorted**, which severely **impairs depth perception and spatial contrast sensitivity**. The **Visible Trial** demonstrated that providing **single-lens distance glasses for outdoor walking and stair navigation** in older adults who habitually wore multifocals reduced fall rates by 40%. (Ref: Visible Trial, BMJ 2010; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines)'),
