    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Richard with a history of hypertension and osteoarthritis is prescribed progressive glasses to manage concurrent presbyopia and myopia. He presents to the clinic after sustaining a fall while walking down his basement stairs. He reports that he misjudged the height of the step. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical reasoning regarding the relationship between his eyewear and his fall?', '["The lower segment of multifocal/bifocal lenses is designed for near vision; looking through it while walking distorts depth perception and spatial awareness of the lower visual field (e.g., stairs, curbs), increasing fall risk", "Multifocal glasses cause transient horizontal nystagmus by stimulating the vestibular system during vertical eye movements", "The fall was unrelated to the glasses and was entirely caused by age-related peripheral motor apraxia", "The patient should wear stronger reading glasses while walking to improve visual acuity of distant stairs and obstacles", "Multifocal lenses cause chronic pupillary constriction, which impairs dark adaptation and leads to sudden autonomic syncope on stairs"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Multifocal, bifocal, and progressive lenses** are a major, often overlooked, modifiable risk factor for falls in older adults. The lower portion of these lenses is corrected for near vision (reading distance). When an older adult looks down through the bottom of the lens while walking or navigating stairs, the ground and steps appear **blurred and distorted**, which severely **impairs depth perception and spatial contrast sensitivity**. The **Visible Trial** demonstrated that providing **single-lens distance glasses for outdoor walking and stair navigation** in older adults who habitually wore multifocals reduced fall rates by 40%. (Ref: Visible Trial, BMJ 2010; Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS/BGS Fall Prevention Guidelines)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old female named Marie with mild cataracts and presbyopia reports increasing difficulty walking in the evening, stepping off curbs, and reading text printed on colored paper. She has 20/30 visual acuity on the Snellen chart in bright light. Which of the following visual parameters is impaired in this patient, and what is its functional consequence?', '["Contrast sensitivity (the ability to detect subtle differences between target and background); impairment increases the risk of falls on stairs, curbs, and uneven surfaces, especially in low-light conditions, even if Snellen visual acuity is relatively preserved", "Kinetic visual field; leading to selective loss of peripheral vision and bitemporal hemianopsia", "Glare recovery; causing immediate permanent pupillary dilation and corneal clouding", "Intraocular pressure accommodation; leading to optic nerve head cupping and decreased tear production", "Color vision (trichromacy); causing selective loss of red-green perception due to rod degeneration"]'::jsonb, 0, '**Contrast sensitivity** is the ability of the visual system to distinguish an object from its background (e.g., detecting a grey step against a grey floor). It naturally declines with age due to changes in the lens (cataracts/increased light scattering) and retina (loss of ganglion cells). A patient can have **near-normal Snellen visual acuity (e.g., 20/30) in high-contrast, bright clinical settings** but still have severe functional impairment in low-contrast, real-world settings (e.g., dim light, fog, rain). Poor contrast sensitivity is strongly associated with **recurrent falls, driving accidents**, and difficulty performing activities of daily living (ADLs). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Ophthalmology 2011)'),
