    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male named Joseph with mild cataracts and presbyopia reports increasing difficulty walking in the evening, stepping off curbs, and reading text printed on colored paper. He has 20/30 visual acuity on the Snellen chart in bright light. Which of the following visual parameters is impaired in this patient, and what is its functional consequence?', '["Kinetic visual field; leading to selective loss of peripheral vision and bitemporal hemianopsia", "Intraocular pressure accommodation; leading to optic nerve head cupping and decreased tear production", "Color vision (trichromacy); causing selective loss of red-green perception due to rod degeneration", "Contrast sensitivity (the ability to detect subtle differences between target and background); impairment increases the risk of falls on stairs, curbs, and uneven surfaces, especially in low-light conditions, even if Snellen visual acuity is relatively preserved", "Glare recovery; causing immediate permanent pupillary dilation and corneal clouding"]'::jsonb, 3, '**Contrast sensitivity** is the ability of the visual system to distinguish an object from its background (e.g., detecting a grey step against a grey floor). It naturally declines with age due to changes in the lens (cataracts/increased light scattering) and retina (loss of ganglion cells). A patient can have **near-normal Snellen visual acuity (e.g., 20/30) in high-contrast, bright clinical settings** but still have severe functional impairment in low-contrast, real-world settings (e.g., dim light, fog, rain). Poor contrast sensitivity is strongly associated with **recurrent falls, driving accidents**, and difficulty performing activities of daily living (ADLs). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Ophthalmology 2011)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 93-year-old female named Edith presents for a routine physical. Her family reports that she recently stopped driving at night because she was ''blinded'' by the headlights of oncoming cars, taking several seconds to recover her vision. What is the physiological basis of this sensory change, and what is the best clinical recommendation?', '["Accelerated degeneration of the outer segment of macular cones; prescribe high-dose vitamin A and recommend driving only during twilight", "Excessive accumulation of rhodopsin in the vitreous humor; prescribe topical miotic drops to induce miosis", "Increased glare sensitivity and delayed glare recovery due to light scattering by age-related lens opacities (cataracts) and slower pupillary constriction/adaptation; recommend formal driving assessment and counsel on avoiding night driving", "Severe atrophy of the optic tract; refer for emergent neurosurgical decompression", "Age-related loss of visual pathway myelin; reassure the patient that this is normal and advise using high-beam headlights continuously"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Age-related ocular changes, particularly **cataracts** (which cause light to scatter across the retina) and **miosis** (smaller resting pupil size) coupled with **slower pupillary light reflex/adaptation**, lead to **increased glare sensitivity and prolonged glare recovery time**. When exposed to a bright light source (such as oncoming headlights at night), older adults are functionally blinded for several seconds. This represents a significant **driving safety hazard**. The best recommendation is to evaluate for cataracts, optimize corrective lenses, and counsel the patient to avoid night driving. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 44; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
